[node name="Fighter Flavor Text" type="Label" parent="."]
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 485.0
offset_top = 112.0
offset_right = 1135.0
offset_bottom = 537.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "     The backbone to any combat unit, the Fighter is indispensable. Though other classes may be 
concerned with leveraging religious favor or magical manipulation of the battlefield, Fighters concern 
themselves with tactics. Armaments, formations, flanking, ambush; these are the bread and butter 
of the Fighter.
     A Fighter wishes to meet his enemy in hand to hand combat and is unlimited in weapon choice. 
Similarly, a Fighter can use any pieces of armor he can afford or find. Fighters are awarded for 
prioritizing Strength and Constitution, though Dexterity is not a waste. Sturdy and proficient in melee, 
Fighters establish the frontline in battle and are necessary to exert a line of control.
     No other class enjoys as many advantages when it comes to standing your ground and going blow 
for blow with the enemy. The moniker of Fighter spans from common farmer armed and raised by a 
lord into soldiery to dedicated knight trained with sword and shield since an early age. As such, 
Fighters are ubiquitous across the world. No matter the culture or creed, every successful civilization 
has a reliable force of Fighters."

[node name="Fighter Bonuses" type="Label" parent="."]
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 14.0
offset_top = 303.0
offset_right = 475.0
offset_bottom = 608.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "Statistical and Ability Details for the Fighter:

- Gain 1d10 Hit Points upon Level-up.

- +1 to Initiative Rolls (Initiative is a 1d10 roll).

- Gain a Weapon Proficiency every Level-up.

- Gain an extra attack, every other round, at Levels 5, 9, and 13.

- THAC0 improves at a rate of 1 per Level.

- Access to all Weapons and Armors without penalty.
"

[node name="Paladin Flavor Text" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 477.0
offset_top = 85.0
offset_right = 1144.0
offset_bottom = 632.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "     Paladins are the embodiment of divine retribution. Disinterested in the theological debate that 
effuses monasteries and temples, the Paladin is interested only in enacting religious justice on his 
environs. Such rigid ideology inevitably leads to conflict and the Paladin revels in the opportunity to 
solve such conflict with violence. As such, Paladins are accomplished warriors, and the divine powers 
with which they are equipped are attuned to such an end.
     Paladins are typically made one of two ways. The first is internally within a monastery or temple. 
A Priest, or more rarely Cleric, becomes so frustrated with the lifestyle and relative inaction of the 
peaceable church that he/she decides to strike out from such comforts in search of areas where 
discordance is the norm. Once in an area of evil, the Paladin is able \"to do the most good\" by stamping 
out wickedness. Outfitting a Paladin is no small expense and a temple will not do so without the 
candidate proving unusually gifted in combat. 
     The second, and more likely scenario, is that of the knight. Trained in warfare from an early age, 
the knight turned Paladin has an unusual conscious. One that is burdened by waging war on the whim 
of a lord. In this moral crisis the knight swears himself to the church and a Paladin is born. 
     Though either scenario may paint the Paladin in a negative light, let it be known that the status of 
Paladin cannot be acquired falsely. Just as in Priests and Clerics, Paladins share a divine relationship 
with a Deity, from whom their powers are derived. If this relationship is ever reneged upon, the status 
of Paladin is swiftly revoked. Due to the extreme nature of a Paladin's mandate, Paladins are held to an 
abnormally high standard by their Deity, and very little deviation is tolerated. Any Paladin revoked 
of his/her status immediately loses all divine powers and is reduced to a Fighter at half his/her previous 
level. Remediation can be attempted but success is rare.
     With such strict edicts and theology, Paladins are often revered and simultaneously feared. Most 
persons shy away from Paladins and this usual reaction only differs in times of extreme need. When such 
dire situations do arise, a Paladin is treated much like a King, with villagers and lords alike 
acquiescing to their every request."

[node name="Paladin Bonuses" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 12.0
offset_top = 289.0
offset_right = 461.0
offset_bottom = 638.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "Statistical and Ability Details for the Paladin:

- Gain 1d10 Hit Points upon Level-up.
- +2 to Saving Throws vs Death, Poison, Paralyzation,
  Petrification & Polymorph.
- Constantly emanate an Aura of Protection to 10ft,
  All enemy combatants receive -1 to-hit.
- The ability to Detect Evil within 60ft at will.
- Immunity to disease and the ability to cure it in others once per week.
- THAC0 improves at a rate of 1 per Level.
- Lay on Hands which heals 3hp per level once a day.
- The ability to Turn Undead at Level 3.
- The ability to cast Spells as a Priest at Level 7.
- The Paladin may never own more than 10 Magical Items.
- The Paladin must tithe 10% of all wealth once per month to a 
  Temple, Monastery or other ecclesiastical body of good alignment."

[node name="Ranger Flavor Text" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 469.0
offset_top = 64.0
offset_right = 1148.0
offset_bottom = 639.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "     The ultimate self-sufficient warrior, the Ranger is accustomed to life on the outskirts of civilization. 
As such, the Ranger hunts his own food, builds his own shelter, and is responsible for his own safety. Each 
day is spent roaming the acreage a Ranger considers home. The Ranger becomes very well accustomed to 
this tract of land, and with little interaction with other intelligent life, the Ranger learns to read the 
wildlife and landscape. What the Ranger cannot produce himself i.e., smithed goods, agricultural 
foodstuffs, oils, etc., he trades for. Most Rangers are accomplished hunters, and the hides they bring to 
market are often of the best quality around.
     Rangers have other opportunities to make money though. Townspeople will occasionally seek him out to 
look for a lost relative, guardsmen may pay the hunter to track bandits for them, and the astute Ranger 
will know when he is witness to information someone is willing to pay for. Some Rangers are born of the 
local woodsman recruited by local lord to flank his army and act as a scout, spending each night camped 
between the opposing forces. These factors combine to create a compellingly competent individual.
     With so many days spent tracking in the wild, Rangers have developed an attunement to the natural 
world. They move as lithely and quietly as a skilled burglar when in the wild. Such mastery over the body 
extends to the hands, and they are able to dual-wield weapons without penalty (note that the 
encumbrance of heavy armors can negate such agility). Nearly all Rangers cut their teeth with a specific 
enemy and have intimately studied their habits and fighting styles. Rangers are unmatched when fighting 
these foes. Rangers natural attunement extends also to animal life. They understand how to soothe and 
interact with animals implicitly. Rangers even become able to cast limited Druid spells, should they live 
long enough. Whether this too is implicit or aided by an admiring Druid or Dryad, is a secret 
most Rangers take to their grave.
     Such willing isolation does take its toll. The chaos of a city, even a small bustling town, is something a 
Ranger cannot grow accustomed to. Even the unconscious actions of regular people weigh on a Ranger. 
There is no greater pet peeve to a skilled Ranger than the reckless footfalls of a companion that ruin 
the hunt. The Ranger is unable to have, hired or otherwise, any henchmen. This is why you will 
never find a lord, lady or dignitary who is a Ranger."

[node name="Ranger Bonuses" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 22.0
offset_top = 302.0
offset_right = 460.0
offset_bottom = 617.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "Statistical and Ability Details for the Ranger:

- Gain 1d10 Hit Points upon Level-up.

- Dual-wield weapons without penalty unless wearing metal armor.

- Proficiency in Animal Handling, Tracking, and Hunting.

- 10% Hide in Shadows & 15% Move Silently when outdoors, these
  abilities scale with level.

- THAC0 improves at a rate of 1 per Level.

- Access to Plant & Animal Sphere Priest Spells at level 4."

[node name="Thief Flavor Text" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 518.0
offset_top = 116.0
offset_right = 1152.0
offset_bottom = 459.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "     Crafty, clever, and opportunistic the Thief comes in a variety of flavors. Some are forced into 
larceny at an early age and grow accustomed to getting by with sleight of hand. Others find this 
calling via organized banditry after falling on tough times. Some Thieves even have an honest
conscious, being taught their craft by dungeon explorers and woodland scouts. However the art is 
learned, the Thief has an extensive toolkit able to tackle an extensive variety of situations.
     Often quick-witted when faced with hostility, the Thief specializes in disguising intentions until 
the situation can be manipulated into his/her favor. If forced into hand-to-hand combat, the Thief's 
unusual hand-eye coordination makes them a tricky combatant, but attack from concealment is the
Thief's preferred method should violence prove necessary. A dagger from the dark is particularly 
deadly when wielded by a Thief.
     Within a party, the Thief's value is in his/her utility. In a lead role, the Thief can ply ahead of the 
party checking for traps and concealed corridors. Locked chests and doors can be opened with pins 
and hooks. Closed doors and blind corners can be listened to with an acute ear. Some Thieves even 
master the art of code-breaking. These skills, combined with the ability to lie concealed when setting 
ambushes, make Thieves an integral part of many an adventuring party."

[node name="Thief Bonuses" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 9.0
offset_top = 293.0
offset_right = 504.0
offset_bottom = 642.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "Statistical and Ability Details for the Thief:

- Gain 1d6 Hit Points upon Level-up.

- Thieving Skills: Pick Pockets, Open Locks, Climb Walls, Move Silently,
  Hide in Shadows, Detect Noise, Read Languages Find/Remove Traps.
  The starting values of these abilities is influenced by Race and Dexterity.

- An additional +2 to-hit when attacking from the flank or behind.
- An additional action in the surprise round of combat.
- Ability to Backstab for double damage when wielding a dagger type weapon.
  This multiplier increases by 1 every 4th Level-up.
- THAC0 improves at a rate of 1 every other Level.
- The ability to use scrolls based on Read Languages percentile.
- Prohibited Weapons: Great Weapons and Polearms. 
- Inability to wear Metal Armors."

[node name="Bard Flavor Text" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 492.0
offset_top = 97.0
offset_right = 1153.0
offset_bottom = 522.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "     Able to entertain an entire village one night and an altogether forgetful traveler the next, Bards bear
a curious niche in society. Bards are experts at playing a \"role\". That role is often whatever is most 
advantageous to the Bard at that exact moment in time and can change day to day or even conversation
to conversation. Keeping each of these roles afloat, without having them crash into and contradict one 
another, is what separates a talented Bard from the local dungeon.
     It is true that many a Bard pays for meals and lodging with an act, whether it be poetry, song,
acrobatics, or some other rehearsed talent. A good Bard, under the proper circumstances and 
given adequate time to perform, can even inspire a group before battle. While such theatrical ability is 
laudable, this outward facing persona does not detail a Bard's occupation.
     A Bard is skilled in many the same ways a Thief is, and can perform the role adequately should need 
arise. Bards also have the Intelligence and finesse required for some rudimentary ability with Magic and 
can cast a limited number of spells in addition to cast scrolls.
     In combat a Bard is similarly varied in experiences and is only barred from using great weapons. A
Bard is also able to wear any armor excepting Plate. Bards are widely bilingual, and learned in their 
culture's local history. Bards also have some ability to Identify magical items. Such ability grows 
alongside a Bard's Spell Level. These boons are attributed to a Bard's familiarity with various cultures."

[node name="Bard Bonuses" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 11.0
offset_top = 295.0
offset_right = 474.0
offset_bottom = 644.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "Statistical and Ability Details for the Bard:

- Gain 1d6 Hit Points upon Level-up.

- Thieving Skills: Pick Pockets, Open Locks, Climb Walls, Move Silently,
  Hide in Shadows, Detect Noise, Read Languages Find/Remove Traps.
  Starting values of these abilities is influenced by Race and Dexterity.

- Access to Mage spells from first level.
- Fluency in High Cant as well as a language of the Bard's choice.
- A musical instrument of choice, the ability to play it and sing.
- The ability to Inspire before momentous battles:
  Conveys +1 to Saving Throws, +1 to To-Hit, and +2 to Morale.
- THAC0 improves at a rate of 1 every other Level.
- The ability to use Scrolls and learn from Spell Tomes.
- Ability to use all armor types and all weapons but Great."

[node name="Priest Flavor Text" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 471.0
offset_top = 119.0
offset_right = 1137.0
offset_bottom = 544.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "     Priests derive their spell-casting abilities from divine favor. The varieties of such a class are therefore 
as numerous as the Deities worshipped across the expanse of the world. The customs of the Priest are 
entirely based upon the bestowing Deity. The spells available, the customs of worship, and the raiment 
and weapons allowed are all dependent on this relationship.
     That being said, some generalities can be laid out. Priests are primarily a spell casting class. They are 
granted freedom to all available spells without having to pre-select a number of casts to allot. These Spell 
Casts are replenished by ritual worship to their Deity. The exact nature of this ritual varies from Deity 
to Deity. Deities are associated with specific magical \"spheres\" (plant, elemental, healing, etc). Most 
Deities have a primary sphere and one or two secondary spheres. Spells from the primary sphere will be 
accessible to the Priest first.
     Many Priests begin and end their adult lives in a cloister or monastery. Worship is their primary work 
in life and many Priests spend entire days of their lives wrapped in the study and reproduction of 
religious texts. Such devotion ties a Priest to his/her God closely, but most Priests are expected to give 
service to their community as well. Depending on the Deity in question Priests may be called upon to 
bless fields, purify wells, or heal the sick. Such services do not come freely however. Priests expect 
recompense through tithes to the Temple, tithes which fund the often modest lifestyles of the Priests 
residing therein."

[node name="Priest Bonuses" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 22.0
offset_top = 304.0
offset_right = 460.0
offset_bottom = 631.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "Statistical and Ability Details for the Priest:

- Gain 1d6 Hit Points upon Level-up.

- Access to all Spells within the Spheres of the chosen Deity.

- Three 1st level Spell Casts at first level.

- Proficiency in Reading/Writing, Ancient History, and Religion.

- Armor and Weapon restrictions determined by Deity.

- THAC0 improves at a rate of 1 every third Level.

- The ability to use Scrolls and learn from Spell Tomes."

[node name="Cleric Flavor Text" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 476.0
offset_top = 116.0
offset_right = 1142.0
offset_bottom = 541.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "     The life of a Cleric is that of a traveler. Called upon to spread the word and works of their Deity, the 
Cleric is oft found living the rough life of the traveler with little more than a roughspun robe and 
quarterstaff. As befits such a lifestyle, Clerics tend to be practical folk. They attempt to fund this lifestyle 
through acts of faith but are just as likely to be seen earning a meal chopping wood or carrying water.
     Clerics, much like Priests, are devoted to a specific Deity and inherit all the same limitations of spell 
\"sphere\". Clerics are afforded more leniency insofar as the specificity of their rituals, but are more limited 
in spell selection. Clerics must choose what spells they are gifted as they level whereas Priests are granted 
full access to all spells of the appropriate sphere and spell level upon level-up. Clerics are also 
considerably more adept in a fight and are able to wear metal armor without disrupting their ability to 
cast spells.
     With such avant-garde living Clerics do not have same level of education and schooling as Priests. 
Therefore, most lords & city officials prefer to deal with Priests over Clerics. Priests, with their close ties to 
specific temples, are able to preach with more authority. The average villager, however, considers the 
Cleric a welcome house guest. A hot meal and snug hayloft are happily traded for news of the Clerics 
travels and a quaint blessing when the Cleric parts ways."

[node name="Cleric Bonuses" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 23.0
offset_top = 303.0
offset_right = 463.0
offset_bottom = 630.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "Statistical and Ability Details for the Cleric:

- Gain 1d8 Hit Points upon Level-up.

- Access to chosen Spells within the Spheres of the chosen Deity.

- Two 1st level Spell Casts at first level.

- Proficiency in one of Cooking, Brewing, Fire-building or Agriculture.

- Armor and Weapon restrictions determined by Deity.

- THAC0 improves at a rate of 1 every second Level.

- The ability to use Scrolls and learn from Spell Tomes."

[node name="Druid Flavor Text" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 473.0
offset_top = 107.0
offset_right = 1140.0
offset_bottom = 588.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "     Druids are among the rarest entities that can be encountered in the world. Though originally a person, 
the Druid has become something altogether more primordial. Able to meld into the landscape, speak 
with the foliage, and even shape-shift into animals; the Druid is as foreign to civilization as the most 
remote of hermits.
     Druids are a hierarchical society beholden only to themselves. At the pinnacle of this pyramid is the 
Hierophant, responsible for the equilibrium of the world's many environments. Below him, the sectors of 
land, sea, and air are placed in the hands of three respective underlings. These are then divided again 
among the various climates and so-on until each forest, mountain, and desert has a Druid responsible for 
its well-being. This grand collection is known as the Circle of Druids. In truth, a Druid is not a Druid 
until he/she has taken stewardship for a plot of land. Responsible management of this ecosystem will 
result in official recognition by the Circle.
     Such distinction is not given lightly and Druids spend many years searching for both the appropriate 
place and accruing the necessary worldly experience to nurture such symbiosis. The difference in power 
of a Druid at home in the biome they are responsible for and a Druid still wandering are immense, with 
the former nearly omnipotent and omniscient within its bounds. The latter is similar to a Priest in ability 
but with a few important distinctions.
     The Druid does not have a ritual required for the return of spellcasts beyond a simple directive to 
commune, overnight, with nature. The typical primitive campsite satisfies such a requirement. Druids 
have access to seven of the sixteen spheres: All, Animal, Elemental, Healing, Plant, and Divination. The 
Druid may only use \"natural\" armors, those made of bark or skins. A Druid may only use the following 
weapons: Club, Sickle, Dart, Spear, Dagger, Scimitar, Sling, and Staff. These limitations ensure the Druid
does not become dependent upon civilization for their needs."

[node name="Druid Bonuses" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 12.0
offset_top = 299.0
offset_right = 378.0
offset_bottom = 568.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "Statistical and Ability Details for the Druid:

- Gain 1d8 Hit Points upon Level-up.

- Access to chosen Spells within the Natural Spheres.
- Three 1st level Spell Casts at first level.
- Limited ability to Shapeshift starting at third level.
- Proficiency in Animal Training, Animal Handling, 
  Animal Lore, and Weather Sense.
- Armor and Weapon restrictions detailed in description.
- THAC0 improves at a rate of 1 every third Level.
- The ability to use Scrolls and learn from Spell Tomes."

[node name="Mage Flavor Text" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 472.0
offset_top = 101.0
offset_right = 1145.0
offset_bottom = 560.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("3_su20x")
text = "   Mages are born of an unusual knack, typically found in late adolescense. This original cantrip is the 
Mage's signature. Though at their root the Cantrip is a simple trick, it was a Mage's most instinctual 
way of first discovering their Arcane connection. Therefore, the Cantrip is like a third limb and it can be 
excercised as such. Many a Mage made life's first few big dollars off the back of that trick. From that 
point on the potential of Magic is an undeniable pull and the quest for understanding began.
    While much can be done in many facets of life with will and instinct, Magic is a scholarly art. The 
runic language used to inscribe spells facilitates the evocation of the world's latent energies. Such energies 
are inherent in the fabric of reality; and extend beyond the Material Plane that all the surroundings of the 
physical world exist upon. Each rune has been painstakingly perfected through trial and error extending 
back eons, and possibly moreso. The collection of this runic alphabet, and the many languages it has been 
compiled into, have produced an uncountable amount of spells. They are collected on vellum skins and 
inscribed with enchanted ink. Their very existence the undeniable evidence of times that have come before. 
There are known troves of them in the world and legends surround the ruins that harbor them. Legends of 
apocalyptic devastation but also irrational Arcane Wonders. 
    Magic is also an exact art, It is in the intonation, in the hand movements, a light pinky, a heavy 
shoulder braced against the impact just so. There is a cadence to each and every individual spell. The 
accoustics of your voice interacting with the runes on the page to create a buzz of reality. The channeling 
of that reality performed with the movements of your hands and out through some impetus (fingertip, 
wand, staff). The will of that reality realized then materializes and functions as instructed. 
     The Mage is enthralled. The mastery of such power would be overwhelming in its abundance. 
You could win a war. You could change history. It's all there. You just have to survive the attempt.  "

[node name="Mage Bonuses" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 12.0
offset_top = 291.0
